{"id":"d3bcf011-6e48-4c2d-b9e6-2c6224e8a971","user_id":"9a6760d2-7961-48fa-9317-94c7b086d64d","book_title":"Escape from Pompeii","book_author":"Christina Balit","book_emoji":"📚","subject":"English","year_group":"Year 3","title":"Story Mapping and Retelling","unit_overview":"In this unit, Year 3 pupils explore Christina Balit's 'Escape from Pompeii' to develop their understanding of narrative structure and sequencing. Pupils analyse the key events of the story, practise retelling techniques, and create their own illustrated story map as a planning tool. The unit culminates in a written or oral retelling of the story in sequence, building comprehension, vocabulary, and storytelling skills.","model_example":{"title": "An Illustrated Story Map and Written Retelling of Escape from Pompeii", "sections": [{"label": "Opening", "example": "Long ago, in the great Roman city of Pompeii, a boy named Tranio lived with his actor father near the grand theatre. The streets were full of noise and life, and the towering volcano Vesuvius watched silently over them all.", "placeholder": "Introduce the setting of Pompeii and the main characters, Tranio and Livia. Set the scene of daily life in the busy city."}, {"label": "Build-Up", "example": "One hot August morning, the ground began to tremble beneath their feet. Dogs howled and birds fell silent. Tranio felt a prickle of fear as a strange darkness crept across the bright sky.", "placeholder": "Describe the warning signs that something is wrong — strange tremors, animals behaving oddly and the sky growing dark."}, {"label": "Climax", "example": "Suddenly, with a thunderous roar, Vesuvius exploded into the sky. Burning rocks rained down and a thick cloud of ash swallowed the city whole. Tranio grabbed Livia's hand and they ran through the screaming crowds towards the harbour, their hearts hammering with terror.", "placeholder": "Describe the violent eruption of Vesuvius — the fire, ash, panic in the streets and the desperate race to the harbour."}, {"label": "Resolution", "example": "At last, they scrambled onto a boat and the oarsmen pulled them out to sea. Trembling, Tranio looked back at the city he loved. Pompeii was gone — buried forever beneath a mountain of grey ash.", "placeholder": "Describe Tranio and Livia's escape by boat and their last sight of Pompeii disappearing beneath the ash."}, {"label": "Ending", "example": "Though Pompeii had vanished, it was never truly lost. Hundreds of years later, the city rose again from the ash, and the stories of those who had lived there were told once more.", "placeholder": "Reflect on what Pompeii means now — how it was preserved and remembered. Link to the idea of memory and history."}], "description": "By the end of the unit, pupils will have produced an A3 illustrated story map showing six to eight key events from the story with labelled captions, alongside a written retelling of the story organised in paragraphs and using time connectives and descriptive language."},"lesson_count":6,"created_at":"2026-06-19T12:42:06.538403+00:00","updated_at":"2026-06-19T12:42:06.538403+00:00"}